---
# Every behaviour ends at rest (owner gone, the pane caught up, nothing
# left to bind): that is the end of the flow, not a deadlock.
deadlock_detection: false
---

# One portal (one session, one guest port) seen four ways: the listener
# in the owning bough process (internal/orb/portal.go's portals map),
# the record in ~/.bough/orbs/<session>/state.json, the container and the
# address the listener dials, and the Portal pane (serve/web/src/
# portal.tsx) that polls GET /api/sessions/<id>/orb every 4 s. orb_lifecycle
# reduces all of this to one boolean; this flow is where they disagree.
#
# Where the code disagreed with the mapped model (and the code won):
#   - "OpenRefused" and "ReopenedCached" are not states: a refused open
#     (state.json not running: starting, stopped) and a cached reopen
#     change nothing, so both are no-op branches of AgentOpen.
#   - A cached reopen returns before recordPortal: it never writes
#     state.json, so it cannot repair a record something else dropped.
#   - OpenPortal is two steps with no lock between them and the orb's
#     writes (portals.Lock is not Orb.mu): listen, dialling the IP it
#     read from state.json (AgentOpen), then recordPortal re-reads the
#     file and writes it back with the entry (OpenRecord). The re-read
#     is fresh, so what goes stale is the listener's address, not the
#     file. recordPortal/forgetPortal are also read-modify-writes racing
#     MarkStopped (a lost "stopped"); that window is inside one function
#     and is not modelled.
#   - Orb never carries Portals in its in-memory state (Prepare builds a
#     fresh State; nothing ever sets o.state.Portals), so every write the
#     child makes through the Orb (ensureRunningLocked, resumeLocked,
#     Stop) drops the record. MarkStopped (serve's StopOrb, reaper) reads
#     the file and keeps it.
#   - A restart's new IP reaches the prompt (OnResume) but never the
#     listener: portal.dial is fixed at open.
#   - The pane's "live" is serve's portalViews: a 150 ms TCP dial to
#     127.0.0.1:<host>. It is answered by the owner's listener whatever
#     the container is doing, and by any process that later binds the
#     port. The pane frames whatever answers.
#   - Not modelled: AgentList (tools.portal.list reads state.json and
#     changes nothing: it is `rec`), UserReload/Navigate (remount the
#     frame; the pane's branch is the poll's), a resume of a dead owner
#     (Prepare writes a fresh State, which is StartBy* in orb_lifecycle),
#     and an open in flight when the process ends (the call dies with it).
#
# Fields (role Portal):
#   owner     the session's bough process: live | exited (the row
#             unmounted: CloseSessionPortals + Orb.Stop) | killed (SIGKILL,
#             crash: no cleanup)
#   file      state.json status: running | starting | stopped
#   ctr       the container: running | stopped
#   ln        the owner holds a listener for this guest port
#   lns       listeners for this (session, guest): the map allows one
#   dial_ok   that listener dials the container's current IP
#   rec       state.json lists this portal
#   other     another process has bound the recorded host port (only
#             possible once no listener of ours holds it)
#   pending   an open has listened and not yet written its record
#   moved     the container got a new IP while that open was pending
#   stale     the record names a listener that dials an address the
#             container gave up before the record was written (F4)
#   err_ln    the agent was told open failed while its listener stays open
#   pane      what the Portal pane shows as of its last poll: empty (No
#             portal open) | dead (Closed chip, Nothing answers) | live
#             (frames this session's server) | stalled (framed as live,
#             reaches nothing) | foreign (framed as live, another
#             process's server)
#
# Findings (the ideal invariant, the counterexample fizz gave, and the
# weaker claim the code does keep, which is what is asserted below):
#   F1 "state.json portals == the owner's open listeners" fails three
#      ways: AgentOpen, OpenRecordFails (writeState errored; OpenPortal
#      returns the error with the listener open); AgentOpen, OpenRecord,
#      ExternalStop, Restart (the child's Orb write drops the record, the
#      listener stays, the pane says No portal open, and a reopen is the
#      cached no-op); AgentOpen, OpenRecord, OwnerKilled (the record
#      outlives its listener). Kept: RecordHasItsListener.
#   F2 "a live portal dials the container's current IP": AgentOpen,
#      OpenRecord, StopOrb shows the portal live (the listener answers)
#      with the container stopped: stalled, not dead. Kept:
#      LiveReachesItsServer, with the stopped container carved out.
#   F3 "a portal whose owner is gone is never live" / "a pane never
#      frames another session's server": AgentOpen, OpenRecord,
#      OwnerKilled, OtherBindsPort — the dead owner's port, reused, is
#      shown live and framed. Kept: only a killed owner does this.
#   F4 the open/record gap: AgentOpen, StopOrb, Restart, Resumed,
#      OpenRecord records a listener still dialling the old IP. It is
#      shown live, stalls, and nothing ever moves it to dead (the ideal
#      liveness "after a restart an open portal reaches the new address
#      or shows dead" fails on exactly this lasso). Kept: `stale` names it.
#   F5 "open is idempotent per (session, guest)" holds for listeners
#      (OneListenerPerGuest) but not for the record: see F1.

role Portal:
    action Init:
        self.owner = "live"
        self.file = "running"
        self.ctr = "running"
        self.ln = False
        self.lns = 0
        self.dial_ok = False
        self.rec = False
        self.other = False
        self.pending = False
        self.moved = False
        self.stale = False
        self.err_ln = False
        self.pane = "empty"

    # --- the agent (tools.portal / the native portal tool) ---

    # OpenPortal: refused unless state.json says running (AgentOpen
    # while starting or stopped); an open listener is returned as it is,
    # record or not; else listen on 127.0.0.1:0, dial = state.json's IP.
    atomic action AgentOpen:
        require self.owner == "live" and not self.pending
        if self.file == "running" and not self.ln:
            self.ln = True
            self.lns = self.lns + 1
            self.dial_ok = True
            self.pending = True
            self.moved = False
            self.err_ln = False

    # recordPortal: re-read state.json, replace this guest's entry, write
    # it back. It reads the file afresh, so it records the listener on
    # whatever the file says now — even a restart since the open (F4).
    # The new host port replaces a stale one another process may hold.
    atomic fair action OpenRecord:
        require self.pending
        self.rec = True
        self.stale = self.moved
        self.other = False
        self.pending = False
        self.moved = False

    # writeState failed: OpenPortal returns the error, listener open.
    atomic action OpenRecordFails:
        require self.pending
        self.pending = False
        self.moved = False
        self.err_ln = True

    # ClosePortal: close the listener, forgetPortal rewrites the file.
    atomic action AgentClose:
        require self.owner == "live" and not self.pending and (self.ln or self.rec)
        self.ln = False
        self.lns = 0
        self.forget()
        self.err_ln = False

    # --- the owning process ---

    # The row unmounts: CloseSessionPortals, Orb.Stop (writes stopped
    # with no portals) and the container stops.
    atomic action SessionExit:
        require self.owner == "live"
        self.owner = "exited"
        self.drop_listener()
        self.forget()
        self.file = "stopped"
        self.ctr = "stopped"

    # SIGKILL / crash: the OS closes the listener; state.json stays.
    atomic action OwnerKilled:
        require self.owner == "live"
        self.owner = "killed"
        self.drop_listener()

    # The child's next exec finds the container down
    # (ensureRunningLocked): writes starting from its own State — the
    # record goes (F1).
    atomic action Restart:
        require self.owner == "live" and self.ctr == "stopped" and self.file in ("running", "stopped")
        self.file = "starting"
        self.forget()

    # rt.Start and resumeLocked: a new IP, running, written from the
    # Orb's State again (no portals). The listener still dials the old
    # IP (F4).
    atomic fair action Resumed:
        require self.owner == "live" and self.file == "starting"
        self.ctr = "running"
        self.file = "running"
        self.forget()
        if self.ln:
            self.dial_ok = False
        if self.pending:
            self.moved = True

    # --- serve and the outside world ---

    # Stop orb / the reaper / Kill's stopKilledOrb: MarkStopped keeps the
    # record, the container stops, the listener stays.
    atomic action StopOrb:
        require self.ctr == "running" and self.file == "running"
        self.file = "stopped"
        self.ctr = "stopped"

    # The runtime stops it with nobody writing (engine restart, OOM).
    atomic action ExternalStop:
        require self.ctr == "running"
        self.ctr = "stopped"

    # Another server takes the recorded host port once nothing of ours
    # holds it.
    atomic action OtherBindsPort:
        require self.rec and not self.ln and not self.other
        self.other = True

    # The pane's 4 s poll of the session's orb.
    atomic fair action PanePoll:
        require self.pane != view(self)
        self.pane = view(self)

    # Helpers are atomic: a plain func call is a yield point.
    atomic func drop_listener():
        self.ln = False
        self.lns = 0
        self.dial_ok = False
        self.pending = False
        self.moved = False
        self.err_ln = False

    # A write that leaves this guest's entry out of state.json.
    atomic func forget():
        self.rec = False
        self.stale = False

# The listener reaches this session's server: it dials the address the
# container has now, and the container runs.
def reaches(o):
    return o.ln and o.dial_ok and o.ctr == "running"

# portalViews' live: something accepts on 127.0.0.1:<host>.
def api_live(o):
    return o.rec and (o.ln or o.other)

# What the pane shows after its next poll.
def view(o):
    if not o.rec:
        return "empty"
    if o.ln:
        if reaches(o):
            return "live"
        return "stalled"
    if o.other:
        return "foreign"
    return "dead"

action Init:
    portal = Portal()

# state.json never lists a portal its live owner does not hold. (The
# converse is F1.)
always assertion RecordHasItsListener:
    return portal.owner != "live" or not portal.rec or portal.ln

# One listener per (session, guest): a second open returns the first.
always assertion OneListenerPerGuest:
    return portal.lns <= 1

# A portal shown live reaches this session's server — except with the
# container stopped (F2), a killed owner's reused port (F3) or a record
# written after a restart its listener predates (F4).
always assertion LiveReachesItsServer:
    return not api_live(portal) or reaches(portal) or portal.ctr != "running" or portal.owner == "killed" or portal.stale

# A session that exited cleanly leaves nothing that looks live.
always assertion ExitedNeverLive:
    return portal.owner != "exited" or not api_live(portal)

# Another process's server is framed only on a killed owner's port (F3).
always assertion ForeignOnlyAfterKill:
    return view(portal) != "foreign" or portal.owner == "killed"

# When open fails with its listener still open, the file does not list
# it: the error is at least true of state.json.
always assertion OpenErrorNotRecorded:
    return not portal.err_ln or not portal.rec

# The pane converges on what serve reports.
always eventually assertion PaneCatchesUp:
    return portal.pane == view(portal)

# After the owner goes the pane shows no portal or Closed — unless a
# killed owner's port was reused (F3).
always eventually assertion ClosedAfterOwnerGoes:
    return portal.owner == "live" or portal.pane in ("empty", "dead") or portal.other
